{#
  VRF-Lite handoff for one tenant on one node.

  Adds a VLAN subinterface on the fabric NIC, gives it this tenant's
  point-to-point address, and enslaves it to the VRF OVN-Kubernetes already
  created for the tenant's UDN.

  The port list below is the discovered list PLUS the new VLAN, not just the
  VLAN. NMState rewrites a VRF's ports to exactly what is declared, so
  omitting {{ vrf_fact.ports | join(', ') }} here would detach OVN-Kubernetes'
  own management port from the VRF and take the tenant's pods off the network
  on this node. The values came from vrflite-discover.yml reading the live
  node; they are not guesses and they are not defaults.

  route-table-id is likewise the id OVN-Kubernetes allocated
  ({{ vrf_fact.table }}). Declaring a different one makes NMState recreate the
  VRF instead of editing it, with the same consequence.

  The VLAN interface carries an address and nothing else - no gateway, no
  routes, no DNS. Routes into this VRF arrive over BGP from the leaf.
#}
apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1
kind: NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy
metadata:
  name: vrflite-{{ vrf_fact.tenant }}-{{ vrf_fact.ip_key }}
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    kubernetes.io/hostname: {{ vrf_fact.node }}
  capture:
    fabric-nic: interfaces.mac-address=="{{ clab_fabric_mac_prefix | upper }}:{{ ip_list[vrf_fact.ip_key] }}"
  desiredState:
    interfaces:
      - name: "{{ '{{' }} capture.fabric-nic.interfaces.0.name {{ '}}' }}.{{ vrf_fact.vlan }}"
        type: vlan
        state: up
        mtu: {{ clab_fabric_mtu }}
        vlan:
          base-iface: "{{ '{{' }} capture.fabric-nic.interfaces.0.name {{ '}}' }}"
          id: {{ vrf_fact.vlan }}
        ipv4:
          enabled: true
          dhcp: false
          address:
            - ip: {{ vrf_fact.vrf_prefix }}.{{ ip_list[vrf_fact.ip_key] }}
              prefix-length: {{ clab_fabric_netmask_bits }}
        ipv6:
          enabled: false

      - name: {{ vrf_fact.vrf }}
        type: vrf
        state: up
        vrf:
          route-table-id: {{ vrf_fact.table }}
          port:
{#
  The discovered list MINUS this tenant's own VLAN subinterface, then that
  subinterface added back explicitly.

  Dropping it first is what makes the policy idempotent. On a first run the VRF
  holds only ovn-k8s-mpN, so the discovered list and the appended VLAN do not
  overlap. On a RE-RUN the VLAN is already enslaved, discovery returns it like
  any other port, and restating it alongside the append gives NMState the same
  interface twice:

      NmstateError: InvalidArgument: Controller blue (vrf) has multiple ports
      pointing to the same kernel interface enp8s0.110

  which fails the whole policy. The symptom is confusing because a NEW tenant
  applies cleanly in the same run - it has no VLAN yet - so the failure looks
  tenant-specific rather than like the re-run it is.

  Matching on the '.<vlan>' suffix rather than on the full name because the
  appended entry is an NMState capture expression, resolved to the real device
  name at apply time and not comparable here. OVN-Kubernetes' own ports are
  ovn-k8s-mpN and carry no dot, so nothing else can match.
#}
{% set vlan_suffix = '.' ~ vrf_fact.vlan %}
{% for p in vrf_fact.ports if not p.endswith(vlan_suffix) %}
            ## Pre-existing port, owned by OVN-Kubernetes - restated so
            ## NMState does not remove it.
            - {{ p }}
{% endfor %}
            - "{{ '{{' }} capture.fabric-nic.interfaces.0.name {{ '}}' }}.{{ vrf_fact.vlan }}"
